WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
[Narrator:] The following program is brought to you in living color by NBC.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:35.180
[Music]

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:49.100
[Baby tosses and turns while crying in his
crib.]

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.860
[Mother:] Okay, Okay!

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.440
Here's your bottle.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:01:01.640
[Rat scampers around on the floor while baby
is being fed.]

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:18.900
[Baby crying.]

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.780
[Woman runs out of the house screaming.]

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.140
[Narrator:] Not a pretty sight is it?

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.200
No, rat bites never are.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.880
But treating rat bites, or all the diseases
rats cause is one thing,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.120
preventing them is another.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.400
That's where I come in.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:38.900
I'm Jeff Gannon.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.240
My job, Director of Rat Control for the city
health department.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.340
At least that's my official title.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.720
Mostly, people simply call me the rat man.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.440
That's Joe Barnes just leaving.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.120
He's new on the job, but turning into a good
rat man himself.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.120
And so is George Wilson there.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.020
Sometimes George and I work together, most
times we don't, but either way he's tops.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.760
Been at it twenty years.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.780
[George:] Another rat bite, Jeff?

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.360
[Jeff:] A baby again.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.200
Mercy Hospital.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.960
Multiple bites on the face and hands.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.840
It shouldn't bother me after five or six years...but,
it should never happen.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.620
[George:] Don't let it get you down.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.320
Think of all the cases that don't happen because
you're in their pitching.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.460
[Narrator:] Well, as usual our work was waiting.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:39.120
All cases of one kind or another that we have
to investigate before we can send a crew out.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:40.220
Where?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.540
Most everywhere.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:48.420
We get the word from hospitals or the police,
from homeowners, store owners, or tenants,

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.780
anybody who calls in for just one reason.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:53.640
He's got rats.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.440
[Young lady:] No sir, my mother wasn't home
when the baby got bit.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.360
She works and I keep the baby.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.240
[Jeff:] But if you were with the baby, how
come?

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.880
[Young lady:] I mean, I just gave her a bottle.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.540
There's nothing wrong with that, is there?

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.940
I can't watch her every minute.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.540
[Neighbor:] You know mister, the kids around
here don't care.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.980
They don't care one bit.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.340
Did you hear about all those rats?

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.100
Well, we sure got 'em around here.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.380
[Jeff:] I can see that, and apparently the
kids aren't the only ones who don't care.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.840
[Narrator:] And that suits the rats just fine.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.460
Listen to the rat man, all they want is a
place to live.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.200
A messy place, where they can gnaw their way
through doors or loose screens

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.760
or jump right in through broken windows, holes
around pipes or in the walls.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.680
Just enough room for a runway between their
food and their shelter.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.940
A place to bore into the ground and come up
inside a building.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.100
Enough rubbish to build their nests and have
another litter in less than a month.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.080
And what they want most of all is enough to
eat.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.780
There's an old saying in my business "If you're
feeding 'em, they're your rats."

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.940
They'll eat most anything too.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.500
Even spoiled food that would make people sick.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:24.740
For them, garbage is a well-balanced diet.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.440
And the closer that food is, the better.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.580
Rats don't like to travel far.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:31.940
Or in the open.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:33.680
Or in the daytime.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:34.940
Too dangerous.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.420
Just grab it quickly and then hide.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.080
That's the way they want it.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:40.680
Rats are smart.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.720
[Jeff:] Okay ma'am I'll notify the owners,
and then get a crew out here to set up poison

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:47.240
bait

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:48.820
and try doing some ratproofing.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.100
Meanwhile, you folks in the neighborhood better
start cleaning up.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.300
Or else these rats will be hanging around
on the streets.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.920
[Woman:] Well I don't know, it sure ain't
going to be easy but we'll try.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.940
You know, they've always lived here and probably
always will.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.160
[Narrator:] Sure, they've always lived here,
and why not?

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.420
Almost like ringing the dinner bell for every
rat in town.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.560
Exactly what they need and want to set up
housekeeping.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.040
Rats go through the drums for you.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.760
Too big for the city to handle, no lids.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.740
Shouldn't be used for garbage in the first
place.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.580
[Resident:] Something the matter, rat man?

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:33.140
[Jeff:] Yes.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.260
That's a real mess you've got there.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.420
Why don't you get good cans, with lids.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.100
[Resident:] Not my problem, I just live here.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.820
[Jeff:] Sure you just live here, you and the
rats.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:53.500
[Manager:] Rats?

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:55.660
Heck yeah, we got rats.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.940
Man it's like I told you, I'm not the owner. I'm
just the manager.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.420
Besides, ain't nothing I can do.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.080
[Jeff:] Mister, I've given you two warning
notices, now you get a penalty ticket.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.260
Once the owner pays the fine in court, you
can bet he'll tell you to start acting like

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.180
a real manager.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.140
[Restaurant Owner:] Look, I don't want rats
in my restaurant.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:21.300
You think I'm crazy?

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.600
If the city would do its job I wouldn't have
'em.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:28.900
[Jeff:] The city does what it can, but the
citizen is in this thing, too.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.900
We can't keep house for you.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.060
[Resident 2:] This used to be a good place
to live.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.340
Look at it now.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:42.040
Before these ole rats started coming around.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:46.020
[Jeff:] Don't blame the rats lady, don't blame
anybody.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:51.000
Just keep in mind that it takes a bit of effort
on everyone's part to keep a neighborhood

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.680
clean and neat so you won't have rats.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.660
[Narrator:] Oh yes, there are thousands of
rat bite cases every year.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.840
Most of them infants with food on their faces
after eating.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.840
Or, older persons.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.940
If we get them promptly to treatment, most
wounds usually heal satisfactorily.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.960
Unfortunately, many victims also suffer from
the agony and pain of rat-bite fever.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.339
[Jeff:] Biological warfare, rats versus people.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.000
You betting on the people, George?

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.100
[George:] Sure, just the same as you are.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.140
Just like you say, war is everybody's business.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:33.120
It has to be.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.620
That's why more of 'em have got to join up.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.620
[Jeff:] Right, and if cats were the rat- catchers
people think they are,

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:44.340
we wouldn't be mixing tons of poison bait,
ratproofing buildings, or forever holding clinics.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:46.900
[George:] Cats, dogs.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.120
what's the difference?

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.680
They're not much better than traps really.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:52.720
Like the butcher shop case.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.800
Ahh, that was before your time, Jeff.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:57.499
[George narrating:] It was over on the west
side.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.540
And the butcher used traps, and he caught
a rat now and then...

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.700
so he figured he had the problem licked.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.220
Most people do.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.659
But man, the ones he didn't catch must have
had a picnic every night.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.540
Why, with all those scraps, it's a wonder
he ever caught a rat in a trap.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.820
This was all in the backroom, of course,

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.540
so the customers had no reason to suspect
a thing.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.200
Not then anyway.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:23.220
Most people expect places that handle food
to be clean as a whistle.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.700
But they sure knew something was wrong when
that food poisoning hit 'em.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:29.200
And I mean them!

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.100
Men, women, kids.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.160
Why that food was so contaminated from rat
droppings left on his table,

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:38.300
that butcher could've poisoned a small army.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.920
On top of losing his customers, he got a good
case of lepto all on his own.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.400
Probably from infected rat urine, and the
mop water got right into his system.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.500
Anyhow, he sure got the message.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.780
[Jeff:] Leptospirosis huh?

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.080
Complete with chills, vomiting, muscular aches,
that’s pretty serious.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.520
[George:] You better believe it.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.680
[Dr. Lee:] I am Dr. C. Bruce Lee of the research
and development office

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.640
of the Environmental Control Administration
of the US Public Health Service.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:23.880
We have with us today Mr. Harry B. Pratt,
Chief of the Rodent Control Branch

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.720
of the Environmental Control Administration.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:28.220
How are you, Mr. Pratt?

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.020
[Mr. Pratt:] Dr. Lee, it's a pleasure to be
here today.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.779
Uh, we plan to use this motion picture in
many places throughout the country

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.460
where there is a rat problem.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:44.340
But particularly in the twenty cities which
are funded under the Partnership for Health program.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.760
[Dr. Lee:] Could you tell us something, where
these cities are located?

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:52.980
[Mr. Pratt:] Well as you can see on the map,
these twenty cities are scattered from coast to coast.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:10:01.300
Uh, including such very large cities as Chicago,
Cleveland, New York, and Washington.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.680
[Dr. Lee:] How much money is the federal government
allocating

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:05.930
for this rat control program?

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:12.300
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, in the first year of operations, the federal government has spent about fifteen million dollars.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.500
particularly in places like Chicago, New York,
and Baltimore

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.240
where there are existing programs,

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:25.640
and in certain places like Atlanta and Nashville
are where the money is used to start new programs.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.580
[Dr. Lee:] Can you uh, tell us some information? Does
the program cover an entire city?

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:32.520
[Mr. Pratt:] No.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.580
Usually there's not enough money to cover
an entire city,

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:41.840
so the work is concentrated for effective
rat control in the low-income areas.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:45.620
Quite often in the Model Cities or in the
Model Neighborhood area.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.620
[Dr. Lee:] Now that's interesting.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.440
In these areas, who does the actual work on
the program?

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:57.000
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, each program is planned
and directed by trained engineers, biologists,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.960
and sanitarians with broad experience in insect
and rodent control work

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.140
and uh very frequently, years of work with
the health department.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:10.940
However, the majority of the workers are recruited
from the inner city where the control work

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:12.160
is being carried out.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:16.120
[Dr. Lee:] You mean to say that we have people
working on the program

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.790
who have grammar school educations and who
have been to high school?

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.620
Are they working on these activities?

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.600
[Mr. Pratt:] Yes, as you can see here, uh,
some of these men are dropouts

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.340
or people who could not go further in school
because of lack of money.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.620
However, they can be trained to do very fine
work in a number of types of jobs,

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:37.100
such as collecting rubbish.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:43.660
Or they can be used to observe rat signs,
as you see on the corner of this door here.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.980
Uh, rats' teeth grow four or five inches a
year and they can gnaw through many building materials,

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.240
even lead pipe with their sharp teeth.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.640
Notice along the edge of this building, a
rat runway.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:04.520
Rats feel comfortable when they're using the
same path day after day between their homes

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:05.879
and the source of food.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:11.020
[Dr. Lee:] These signs are very interesting,
of rats being about, but what else do the men

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.040
who are engaged in the program look for when they make an inspection?

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.579
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, of course the most important
thing, we feel, is the checking

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:23.060
to see about refuse storage and garbage,

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:32.120
because garbage and refuse furnish the food
and nesting material for the rat.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:36.640
It's amazing how many garbage cans have no
bottoms or tops.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:40.360
There's no place for the bottomless or topless
fad in refuse storage.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.340
[Dr. Lee:] Well, since we have no place for
the bottomless or topless fad in refuse storage,

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:51.000
what do the control workers do when they find
garbage cans which are unsatisfactory?

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:56.200
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, of course the first thing
they do is to come up and meet the householder

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:58.840
and try to get him to buy new garbage cans.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:04.320
Uh they try to get rid of old appliances,
old automobiles, places where rats

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:06.300
would hide in the backyard.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.780
This is probably the most important thing
that each householder can do,

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:15.560
is to have good refuse storage on his own
property.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:21.480
[Dr. Lee:] Well this is important to note,
we must have good storage places

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.140
for refuse, on property.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.860
Now, are rats confined to particular areas
of the city?

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.240
[Mr. Pratt:] We can start in the sewers.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:35.280
So these sewers, then, serve as a place where
the rats can hide and they can travel.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:40.080
And of course they can come up into some of
our very best houses right through the floor

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:40.780
drains.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:44.800
They can even crawl and swim through a toilet,
into our basements.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:50.460
[Dr. Lee:] Now that's most amazing and it's
sort of chilling to think that even in my

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:51.220
own home...

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:51.760
[Mr. Pratt:] Right...

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:53.320
[Dr. Lee:] I could have a rat coming in by
this means.

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:57.580
Uh of course I am interested in knowing what
are the methods used for killing rats?

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:58.760
How do you go about killing them?

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:04.960
[Mr. Pratt:] Well of course, on the big city
program we use three types of rodenticides,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:06.680
that is, rat-killing chemicals.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:14.100
We use uh, the anticoagulants, we use red
squill, and we use zinc phosphide.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:20.840
Now the anticoagulants are a chemical, which
is mixed usually with yellow cornmeal.

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:22.360
This sort of thing here.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:29.040
Now the rats must eat this for four or five
days consecutively before their blood

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.200
begins to lose the ability to clot, or coagulate,

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:35.620
hence the name anticoagulant.

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:41.380
After this period of time, four or five days
to a week or more, they become weak

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:44.840
and actually die painlessly from loss of blood.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:46.940
Now there's a built-in safety factor.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:52.140
If your child, for instance uh, should happen
to eat this once, or your pet should eat this

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:52.680
once,

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:56.380
there's actually no danger from one feeding.

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:57.940
No sickness, no death.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.420
It must be eaten four or five days in a row.

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:05.240
[Dr. Lee:] Well, it's good to know this, that
there is this built-in safety factor

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.760
and I'm pleased because I do have children.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.870
Now I'm concerned, what would I do if I found
I had rats or mice about my house?

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:17.920
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, of course if you had rats,
the best thing to use are these anticoagulants,

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.880
because of this built-in safety factor.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:25.660
Now they can be bought and used in a yellow
cornmeal mix, it's soft like this,

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:30.240
but it tends to blow, so a number of manufacturers
have made it in a pellet form

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:33.699
which does not blow around nearly as much.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:38.920
This comes probably in fifty or more different
types of commercial formulations.

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:44.079
[Dr. Lee:] This is fascinating to see the
uh, packaging in which these poisons come.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:48.860
I remember as a child that they often said
that a trap was best baited with a piece of

00:15:48.860 --> 00:15:49.360
cheese

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.360
to tickle so a rat's nose, it almost makes
them sneeze.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:55.320
Now, is cheese the best bait to use for traps?

00:15:55.540 --> 00:16:00.660
[Mr. Pratt:] Well of course most housewives
feel that cheese is the best material to use

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:02.110
in baiting a mousetrap.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:07.060
So it'll be done like this and of course you
put the trap on hair-trigger.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:16.040
But actually, we have found that you can get just as many mice or more with apples or peanut butter.

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:20.800
a nutmeat, or many other types of things that
are in our houses, like bacon.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:25.340
[Dr. Lee:] Well then, what do you do about
rats in buildings, for instance?

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:29.699
[Mr. Pratt:] Well of course rats are much
bigger, so we use essentially the same type

00:16:29.699 --> 00:16:31.300
of snap-trap uh,

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.900
which we would set like this.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:37.420
And uh you put your bait here and it would
go like that.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:44.160
On the other hand, if this is the wall now,
like this, we can sometimes trap the rats

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:51.340
by having an expanded trigger of cardboard
or metal as you see here.

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.240
And uh you've got to be very careful Dr. Lee,

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:58.420
because this is uh, something that could catch
your finger very easily.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:03.940
Now if this is the wall, we don't set the
trap this way so that the rat comes in and

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:04.720
is thrown away.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:10.460
We try to get it so that as the rat travels
along the wall that way, why, it is caught

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:11.600
you see,

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:13.280
as it goes along the treadle.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:18.400
Now, there's still another type of trap that
we use that essentially is unbaited.

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:20.340
This is called a steel trap.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:25.540
Now this one again, you can catch your finger in and have a real good little wound.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:30.900
But if you use real caution and set it like
this, and then you come under

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.540
and set it at hair-trigger,

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:36.240
remembering that one jaw of the trap is free,
why then you can do it.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:40.800
Now if this is, again, we don't set it this
way so that if the rat runs along

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.660
it's thrown out, we set it this way.

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:46.320
Then as the rat comes along it's caught in
the trap as you see there.

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:52.680
These, then, are some of the things that we
do as we uh, try to get rid of rats and mice

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:53.680
in our houses.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:57.660
[Dr. Lee:] I'm glad to have you the person
who's demonstrating these traps on the, on

00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:58.840
the program.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.400
But what does all this mean to the average
householder?

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:05.500
[Mr. Pratt:] Well of course to the average
householder uh, this means that

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:10.360
rats are something that they should try to
get rid of as quickly as possible.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:16.480
Now let us look again at our motion picture
and see a little bit more about the urban

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:17.460
rat problem.

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:29.460
[Footsteps]

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.460
[Mr. Barnes:] So the rat is a stealthy, clever
enemy.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.700
He can climb up wires and pipes,

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:45.980
squeeze through half-inch holes,

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.820
jump up and out three feet,

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.320
jump down and out eight feet.

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:54.720
Even drop fifty feet and still survive.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:01.440
He starts fires,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:03.540
destroys food and valuables,

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:05.880
contaminates even more,

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:11.680
and when you multiply the damage of a single
rat by the rat population of the US,

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:17.840
about one rat for every two persons, the yearly
loss skyrockets to more than a billion dollars.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:21.120
So how do you change the picture?

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:24.440
By eliminating a rat here and there, not a
chance.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:30.120
You have to control the entire rat population
and the first step is to eliminate the one

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:30.620
thing

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.360
that supports any population.

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:33.760
Food.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:40.240
A single open garbage can for example, will
support just so many rats, no more.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.240
But add a few more messy cans,

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:46.000
then the mess of a city block,

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.920
plus that of an entire neighborhood,

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:52.200
and suddenly you're supporting a king- sized
rat population.

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:54.700
[Jeff voiceover:] Amen, Mr. Barnes.

00:19:55.780 --> 00:19:58.500
A lot of food, a lot of rats.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:02.100
Less food, less rats.

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:06.220
No food, no rats.

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:13.280
Way I see it, most people are so close to
their rat problem, they overlook the most

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:14.560
obvious solution.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:17.870
Especially when they're blaming somebody else.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:21.840
As always, it boils down to communication,

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:27.200
to telling him point-blank, "Look, if you
want to get rid of your rats, quit feeding them.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:29.920
Quit giving them hiding places.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.920
Rats won't stay in an area where they can't
get food and shelter.

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:38.300
So when you take it away, you take away the
rats."

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:39.800
Here's the idea.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:41.480
You see that?

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:45.460
It's a jumbo-sized, always-closed container

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:49.240
to handle a jumbo-sized garbage and rubbish
problem.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:50.940
And it does just that.

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:54.420
What's more, the city thinks it's great.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:21:00.440
To me, most excuses are cover-ups for doing
nothing.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:06.580
But if anybody, a manager, owner, or tenant
ever had reason to cover up,

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:10.320
it's right inside his garbage can or outside
of it.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:16.380
With just a bit of conscience, a bit of pride,
and a bit of effort on everybody's part,

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:19.940
people can get rid of their rats, and they
will,

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:22.420
if everyone keeps working at it.

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:27.680
[Jeff:] I mean that.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:29.000
Every word of it.

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:35.060
There's no doubt that the rat problem is serious
in every city, large or small.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:38.900
We can't ignore it and we can't wish it away.

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:41.320
But we can do something about it.

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:42.680
Each one of us.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.700
I'm betting on the people in this war on rats.

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:46.960
How about you?

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:57.880
[Truck engine revs]

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:03.180
[Dr. Lee:] Harry, your film says that the
rat problem is serious in this country.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:06.560
Just how serious is the urban rat problem.

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:11.720
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, every big city has a rat
problem.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:18.300
This varies of course with the age of the
housing, with the condition of the housing,

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.660
and with the amount of food and harborage
for rats.

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:25.700
Now, in the map that we showed you before,
in the twenty cities where we examined,

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:30.640
we found about two thousand yards, out of
twelve thousand carefully checked,

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:33.420
had signs of rats outside the home.

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:36.240
This is roughly one-sixth, or sixteen percent.

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:42.300
But even more important uh, sixty-one percent
of the blocks in these cities had at least

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.510
one yard

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:48.100
with signs of exterior, rat signs outside
the home.

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:56.820
[Dr. Lee:] Well these uh, statistics, the
few that you've given us are most revealing,

00:22:56.820 --> 00:23:02.300
and I was wondering does this, are these indications
that give us any uh,

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:08.080
way of knowing what steps you're making in
controlling uh rat, the rat problem?

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:14.520
[Mr. Pratt:] Well, we're making a rather sizable
amount of advance success

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:19.600
in cutting down the number of rat bites and
in cutting down the actual prevalence of rats

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:20.700
in these cities.

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:26.890
Uh for instance in New York City, the number
of rats used to be reported so that there

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:27.660
were

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:29.740
six hundred cases of rat bites a year.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:34.660
Then in a five-year period, from '63 to '67,
uh it dropped down

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:36.530
to about five hundred and fifty.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:41.800
In '68, there were only three hundred and
ninety rat bites reported in greater New York.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.600
And this year we only know about two hundred
thus far.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:48.640
[Dr. Lee:] Well, this does indicate that there
is progress.

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:53.240
I can remember when I was a teacher many years ago, my children, when we were discussing rats,

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:59.960
showed me fingers, and noses, and ears that had been bitten by rats when they were babies.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:04.000
This brought home to me the importance of
control measures.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:08.700
Now I realize that most of the film that we
have seen today has demonstrated

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:13.530
the areas of town that you might say are poor,
which are, where people who live, who don't

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:14.560
have opportunities;

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:16.980
however, rats are found everywhere,

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:17.860
as you said.

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:22.940
And one of the methods of control I assume
is that we must get everybody to cooperate,

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:26.980
meaning the tenants, the residents, and the
people who own the property.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:31.840
It's got to be a...it seems to me, a broad
attack involving everybody

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:35.090
who has anything to do with the environment
in which rats are found.

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:36.320
Would you agree to this?

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:40.280
[Mr. Pratt:] I would think that this is very
definitely true and that this very definitely

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:44.920
has a bearing on another significant part
of the rat problem,

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:48.080
mainly about mental health and well-being.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:54.920
You take children that are brought up in the
inner-city uh, very frequently they grow up

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:58.240
wihtout being able to sleep at night because the rats are up in the ceiling

00:24:58.320 --> 00:24:59.980
or they're gnawing in the walls.

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:04.760
There's a fear of rats and of these ugly,
disfiguring scars.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:10.720
And in some work that we've done it seems
to us that uh, the rats uh, are probably

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:15.740
the number four cause of unrest and turmoil
in the inner-city.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:21.820
Uh, following such things as human relations,
and poverty, and unemployment.

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:26.520
[Dr. Lee:] It's interesting to feel that an
animal, a small animal which has traveled

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:27.670
with man from Asia

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:33.200
to our country, could be a definitive cause
in unrest within our urban centers.

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:38.700
My first experience was reading Hans Zinsser's
book, "Rats, Lice, and History."

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:42.220
This is a rather old book, but it is still
pertinent.

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:48.480
It gives the history of man's reactions, his
relationships to this pest, this neighbor

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:49.000
of man

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:54.760
who has followed without his asking man about
the face of the earth from its origin in Asia.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:26:01.340
And I am more than pleased to realize that
the Environmental Control Administration

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:06.470
and Public Health Service has within it a
program which is concerned with this rat control.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:14.020
Are there, is there any information the viewers
can secure concerning the uh,

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:19.080
control measures uh, the activities that are
being undertaken in rat control.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.560
[Mr. Pratt:] Of course we feel that this is
a people problem

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:24.040
and that we must have the people behind us.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.560
Now New York has done this, they have made
this sign.

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:31.680
Seven things that every New Yorker should
know about their neighbor.

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:37.420
And this brings home to them very clearly
uh, that these are definitely in their homes.

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:40.820
We do a great deal of training with health
departments all over the country

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:43.480
and we have recently produced this book:

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.180
Control of Domestic Rats and Mice,

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:47.760
which is available through the Government
Printing Office.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:52.340
Many health departments and other agencies
are using this with pictures of rats

00:26:52.700 --> 00:27:01.440
and uh trapping, of uh rodenticides and the
refuse storage, all these things.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:05.120
So go to your health department if you have
a rat problem

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:07.460
and get advice from your health department.

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:10.960
[Dr. Lee:] Thank you so much for coming aboard
to talk to us today, Harry.

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:11.640
It's a pleasure.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:12.520
[Mr. Pratt:] My pleasure.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:28:02.120
[Music]

